WEBVTT

00:01.918 --> 00:02.919
TWO WEEKS BEFORE PERSEUS DEPARTS

00:03.002 --> 00:05.046
Huh? What's that, Senku?

00:05.130 --> 00:07.298
Isn't that wheat soaking wet?

00:07.382 --> 00:09.259
It's sprouting already.

00:09.342 --> 00:10.760
It's perfectly fine.

00:10.844 --> 00:13.346
But I'm going to roast it and get it dry.

00:13.430 --> 00:15.265
Why did you soak it
if you were going to dry it?

00:15.348 --> 00:16.933
What is wrong with you?

00:17.017 --> 00:19.352
I'll get the bad bits out with my hands.

00:20.103 --> 00:22.063
And then smash it all!

00:22.147 --> 00:24.899
<i>If we boil and filter these…</i>

00:24.983 --> 00:27.402
I crushed it all. It's like wheat soup.

00:29.362 --> 00:34.034
<i>Here we throw in some Japanese hops</i>
<i>and simmer them.</i>

00:34.117 --> 00:37.037
<i>Then we'll leave the rest</i>
<i>to the great yeast.</i>

00:37.620 --> 00:40.707
It will ferment and turn into alcohol.

00:40.790 --> 00:43.543
It will even get carbonated.

00:50.842 --> 00:53.386
This beer turned out to be great.

00:53.470 --> 00:55.055
It's from ancient Egypt.

00:55.138 --> 00:58.892
It was offered to the pyramid builders.

00:59.476 --> 01:01.519
It's great after doing physical work.

01:01.603 --> 01:02.687
It sure is.

01:05.648 --> 01:06.649
It's awesome!

01:07.692 --> 01:09.986
Are you not going to drink, Senku?

01:10.070 --> 01:13.448
No, I'm fine. It's not just me, though…

01:14.157 --> 01:17.827
If all of us got drunk,
the ship might sink.

01:17.911 --> 01:21.998
It's good that some of us
aren't drinking and are just chilling.

01:23.458 --> 01:26.044
I cannot drink alcohol, can I?

01:26.127 --> 01:27.962
No, you can't.

01:29.464 --> 01:31.341
I understand why.

01:31.424 --> 01:33.384
However, as long as you're on my ship,

01:33.468 --> 01:36.137
when it's time to have fun,
I want everyone to enjoy to their fullest.

01:36.221 --> 01:39.349
I won't leave out a single person.
Francois!

01:40.683 --> 01:41.810
Yes.

01:41.893 --> 01:44.813
For our dear guests
who cannot drink alcohol,

01:44.896 --> 01:48.483
I'll prepare an original
custom non-alcoholic cocktail

01:48.566 --> 01:49.651
that appeals to their tastes.

01:50.235 --> 01:52.946
Are you going that far
with this on-board mini-bar?

01:53.029 --> 01:54.906
Francois is the kind
that goes to that extent.

01:56.449 --> 01:58.576
Let me know if there's anything you need.

01:58.660 --> 02:01.162
Leave it to us, the science team!

02:01.746 --> 02:03.581
I sincerely appreciate it.

02:04.833 --> 02:07.293
In that case,
let me take your word for it…

02:08.002 --> 02:13.466
ORIGINAL WORK: RIICHIRO INAGAKI, BOICHI
SHUEISHA <i>JUMP COMICS</i>

03:44.307 --> 03:45.558
Gum syrup?

03:45.642 --> 03:47.685
It's made out of wheat.

03:47.769 --> 03:49.896
First, I'll wash the flour thoroughly,

03:49.979 --> 03:52.649
and the starch will
accumulate in the water.

03:53.399 --> 03:56.110
And for the secret ingredient,
a bit of sulfuric acid.

03:56.194 --> 03:58.363
Yes, the good old sulfuric acid…

03:58.446 --> 04:00.281
Are you adding
sulfuric acid to our drinks?

04:00.365 --> 04:02.533
There's no way we could drink that, right?

04:03.493 --> 04:05.995
If you drink it crudely,
it would melt your teeth.

04:06.079 --> 04:07.914
That's why I'm simmering it thoroughly.

04:07.997 --> 04:08.831
I see.

04:08.915 --> 04:13.753
So now, using science and all that,
he's going to detoxify it.

04:13.836 --> 04:15.838
Have some patience, Gen.

04:15.922 --> 04:18.049
Now, I'll add the barium carbonate…

04:18.132 --> 04:19.717
-Not another poison!
-Not another poison!

04:20.301 --> 04:22.345
Of course not,
it's just the name that's confusing.

04:22.428 --> 04:25.890
Think about it, there's no way
he'd add real poison to it.

04:25.974 --> 04:27.517
No, it really is poison.

04:27.600 --> 04:28.851
-Why?
-Why?

04:28.935 --> 04:31.187
Shut up already!

04:31.271 --> 04:35.066
I've been making sugar
this way for some time now.

04:35.149 --> 04:39.070
After mixing it well, checking the pH
and filtering it, it is harmless.

04:39.153 --> 04:40.863
This is science.

04:40.947 --> 04:43.074
&lrm;Don't mess around with it,
&lrm;or you'll end up dead.

04:44.033 --> 04:46.411
I present to you gum syrup!

04:49.747 --> 04:51.833
Too sweet!

04:57.046 --> 04:59.048
Are you warming the milk?

04:59.132 --> 05:02.218
But is it enough with just
lukewarm water in this jar?

05:02.302 --> 05:04.137
Yep, that's the point.

05:06.222 --> 05:08.308
It turned into yogurt!

05:08.391 --> 05:12.562
<i>If we add the gum syrup and some lemon</i>
<i>into the filtered yogurt…</i>

05:14.314 --> 05:16.774
YOU FOUND A DAIRY SODA!

05:34.083 --> 05:35.460
Enjoy it.

05:44.052 --> 05:47.346
I had never tried a drink
like this before!

05:47.430 --> 05:51.100
It's super sweet, a bit sour,
and the flavor is awesome!

05:51.184 --> 05:54.228
I just added some apple juice
to the dairy soda.

05:54.312 --> 05:55.980
I like apple-flavored things too.

05:58.066 --> 06:00.359
The cucumber garnish on the rim

06:00.443 --> 06:03.863
has Suika's mask carved on it.
Isn't that cute?

06:04.530 --> 06:06.657
It is not an average decoration.

06:06.741 --> 06:08.284
Drop it inside the glass

06:08.367 --> 06:10.661
once you have drank about half of it.

06:16.918 --> 06:19.629
It tastes like watermelon now!

06:19.712 --> 06:22.423
It's so refreshing!

06:23.007 --> 06:25.760
That's interesting.
How did it go from apple to watermelon?

06:26.844 --> 06:29.430
Isn't it the mix of the juice
with the cucumber?

06:30.431 --> 06:33.017
Like a sunny smile,

06:33.101 --> 06:36.896
the mask sparks a reaction
that shines with a whole new charm.

06:37.980 --> 06:39.857
<i>A cocktail made specially for Suika,</i>

06:39.941 --> 06:42.110
<i>the 2 Side Sunshine.</i>

06:43.611 --> 06:47.031
You're tossing things
just like the bartenders in movies,

06:47.115 --> 06:48.324
aren't you?

06:49.033 --> 06:52.912
Is there any scientific reason
for mixing the drinks that way?

06:52.995 --> 06:54.205
Is there?

06:54.288 --> 06:55.665
There's not a single one.

06:55.748 --> 06:57.041
-Not a single one?
-Not a single one?

06:57.125 --> 06:58.793
It's called a flair.

06:58.876 --> 07:03.172
Its purpose is to delight the guest's eyes
as well as their palate.

07:03.256 --> 07:06.008
You truly care about hospitality.

07:07.677 --> 07:09.804
This is the cocktail I made for Tsukasa.

07:10.847 --> 07:12.140
<i>&lrm;When the red of the pomegranate</i>

07:12.223 --> 07:14.976
is exposed to a scientific light
called blacklight,

07:15.560 --> 07:18.729
it changes like the blaze,
quietly aflame in Tsukasa's heart…

07:19.397 --> 07:20.606
It turned blue!

07:20.690 --> 07:21.816
It's glowing!

07:21.899 --> 07:23.109
It's so pretty!

07:27.613 --> 07:29.991
Not bad at all. It's delicious.

07:30.074 --> 07:34.996
For Gen, beneath its simplicity,
I have got a pinch of mature spice.

07:40.877 --> 07:42.962
What a spicy and mature cola drink!

07:43.045 --> 07:44.714
This one is for Kohaku.

07:45.465 --> 07:48.134
SPICY CINDERELLA

07:49.385 --> 07:50.845
Humans are amazing.

07:51.596 --> 07:55.892
To think we can make this many different
drinks out of ingredients found in nature.

07:57.643 --> 08:00.396
I wonder if all of these
are actually "natural ingredients."

08:00.480 --> 08:04.650
And this is Senku's cocktail.

08:05.318 --> 08:09.614
<i>Starting from the top, there's matcha</i>
<i>whipped cream, milk, and</i> hojicha <i>syrup.</i>

08:10.198 --> 08:14.243
The three colored stripes represent
the layered progression of science.

08:14.327 --> 08:18.331
I guess I can drink it any way I want.
Can I just mix it up?

08:18.414 --> 08:20.208
I thought you'd do that…

08:20.917 --> 08:23.669
If you mix it together gracefully,

08:24.253 --> 08:26.464
the layers of science

08:26.547 --> 08:29.634
will turn into a slightly sweet latte.

08:37.600 --> 08:40.019
Matsukaze, come and drink too!

08:40.603 --> 08:43.189
I want to hear some old tales.

08:43.272 --> 08:47.109
Was I a Lord?
What kind of great man was I again?

08:47.193 --> 08:49.737
That wasn't you, Ginro…

08:51.155 --> 08:55.535
My Lord had a strong will
until the very end.

08:58.162 --> 09:01.040
There were no warning signs at all…

09:02.542 --> 09:06.629
<i>When they started pouring,</i>
<i>I seemed to hear someone's voice.</i>

09:06.712 --> 09:09.465
<i>Perhaps, they stumbled upon it</i>
<i>while they were muttering curses</i>

09:09.549 --> 09:10.591
<i>to the device.</i>

09:11.425 --> 09:13.886
<i>It didn't take long until they realized</i>

09:13.970 --> 09:15.846
<i>how the ancient weapon worked.</i>

09:19.725 --> 09:23.312
If the bandits get to the weapon,
we'll lose control over the island.

09:23.396 --> 09:25.356
We must find and destroy all of them.

09:29.443 --> 09:30.903
My Lord!

09:36.576 --> 09:39.829
Matsukaze… I can't be saved anymore…

09:40.580 --> 09:43.207
My Lord, don't say such nonsense…

09:44.166 --> 09:46.836
I've always wondered…

09:47.962 --> 09:51.882
…why the heavens gave
that petrification weapon to us, humans…

09:53.259 --> 09:55.595
<i>At first I thought</i>
<i>it was some divine punishment,</i>

09:55.678 --> 09:57.221
<i>but it's strange…</i>

09:57.722 --> 10:00.516
<i>If its purpose</i>
<i>was to turn everyone into stone,</i>

10:00.600 --> 10:04.478
wouldn't it have been enough
to cover everything with one device?

10:04.979 --> 10:08.566
And why is it so easy to use

10:08.649 --> 10:10.568
for us, humans?

10:11.694 --> 10:16.157
<i>It seems as if we were being manipulated</i>
<i>to turn each other into stone…</i>

10:17.491 --> 10:19.035
It's so frustrating…

10:19.118 --> 10:23.998
I want to teach the heavens
a lesson, Matsukaze,

10:24.582 --> 10:27.835
to show them that we, humans,
are not foolish beings,

10:28.377 --> 10:31.088
to teach them to not underestimate us…

10:58.157 --> 11:00.076
<i>I guess it's inevitable…</i>

11:00.743 --> 11:02.370
<i>&lrm;I must warn future generations…</i>

11:03.913 --> 11:05.873
<i>about this abominable source of evil.</i>

11:17.093 --> 11:18.344
It's what I know.

11:19.136 --> 11:22.848
The bitter guilt I feel
because I couldn't protect my Lord

11:22.932 --> 11:25.518
won't disappear
even if I took revenge on Why-man…

11:26.602 --> 11:28.938
Still, I…

11:30.856 --> 11:31.857
MATCHA BEER

11:31.941 --> 11:33.526
<i>Beer and matcha.</i>

11:33.609 --> 11:36.237
Its mature bitterness builds up

11:36.320 --> 11:39.115
and slowly evolves into a mellow flavor.

11:39.198 --> 11:40.574
Enjoy it.

11:48.332 --> 11:50.084
-You can't drink!
-You can't drink!

12:04.807 --> 12:08.769
If… If I became stronger…

12:10.479 --> 12:13.816
-Tsukasa, lend me your chest!
-Okay.

12:14.483 --> 12:17.778
Tsukasa… Tsukasa…

12:18.362 --> 12:21.115
I'm sorry for what I did
while I was drunk!

12:21.198 --> 12:22.032
Don't worry.

12:22.783 --> 12:25.661
I was thinking
you'd need to train more, too.

12:27.288 --> 12:30.040
Are we going to wake this one too? Really?

12:31.167 --> 12:35.713
We've got guns and Tsukasa is here.
There's no space left to act out.

12:36.297 --> 12:39.550
We cannot fool around in a moment

12:39.633 --> 12:42.219
when the whole humanity is at stake.

12:42.303 --> 12:45.097
Battles are your strong point, Tsukasa.

12:45.181 --> 12:47.349
If we need you, do as you want.

12:47.975 --> 12:50.019
Since I'm a martial artist,

12:50.102 --> 12:53.689
I can't think of anyone better than him
to be his martial arts instructor.

13:03.365 --> 13:05.701
We want to ask for your help, Hyoga.

13:05.784 --> 13:08.370
If you train him in your modern,
refined martial arts,

13:08.454 --> 13:11.373
Matsukaze will improve quickly.

13:14.210 --> 13:17.046
Yes, but on only one condition…

13:17.963 --> 13:22.134
What? If you expect forgiveness
over your attempt on Tsukasa's life--

13:22.218 --> 13:24.470
That's not it.

13:25.054 --> 13:29.600
The post-restoration justice system
wouldn't approve anyway, right?

13:30.226 --> 13:32.811
All I ask for is permission

13:32.895 --> 13:35.481
to use the Revival Fluid for two people.

13:48.327 --> 13:49.703
Hyoga…

13:54.500 --> 13:57.920
Well, isn't this
the Owari Kan-Ryu spear style?

13:58.003 --> 14:00.923
Since this is my first time ever,

14:01.006 --> 14:02.967
I should go on and practice it.

14:03.050 --> 14:05.844
Before that,
there's something you need to do.

14:07.346 --> 14:08.681
I see.

14:09.598 --> 14:11.892
I see some cute girls here.

14:11.976 --> 14:14.895
They could use a little introduction.

14:14.979 --> 14:17.648
Most definitely, Moz.

14:25.990 --> 14:27.616
Kinro…

14:30.911 --> 14:32.830
I have a bad feeling about this.

14:32.913 --> 14:35.499
Isn't it a bit risky for us
to mingle with those guys?

14:36.292 --> 14:38.752
So, I mean…

14:46.635 --> 14:48.053
Did you know, Kinro?

14:48.137 --> 14:50.890
Even as we pile up our negativity,

14:51.473 --> 14:53.809
it all mellows down in the end.

14:53.893 --> 14:54.768
What do you mean?

14:54.852 --> 14:58.022
That's what Francois said to me.

14:58.105 --> 14:59.273
Is that so?

15:00.983 --> 15:02.401
<i>What's that, Kinro?</i>

15:02.484 --> 15:04.737
<i>-Gotcha!</i>
<i>-Kinro… Ouch!</i>

15:05.988 --> 15:08.365
RYUSUI'S THEATER

15:08.449 --> 15:09.909
At the dawn of the Middle Ages,

15:09.992 --> 15:13.037
the strongest pirates
dominated the North Sea.

15:13.120 --> 15:15.247
Those were us, the Vikings!

15:15.831 --> 15:18.709
With our amazing
Viking sailing techniques,

15:18.792 --> 15:20.961
we arrived at the continent of America

15:21.045 --> 15:23.797
500 years before Columbus.

15:23.881 --> 15:25.925
That's impressive.

15:26.008 --> 15:27.384
How did they manage that?

15:27.468 --> 15:28.636
No fair!

15:28.719 --> 15:32.389
Why are the Vikings the only ones
capable of moving forward that smoothly?

15:32.473 --> 15:34.808
It looks like
there's a landmark over the sea!

15:34.892 --> 15:36.894
The only landmark at sea is the sun.

15:36.977 --> 15:39.980
It's not sunny every day, though.

15:40.064 --> 15:42.024
Vikings are mysterious.

15:42.107 --> 15:45.152
That's where the legend was born!

15:45.819 --> 15:50.157
The Vikings definitely used "sunstones"

15:50.240 --> 15:52.993
which could tell
where the sun was at any time.

15:53.077 --> 15:56.830
Besides, getting your bearings
on the open ocean

15:56.914 --> 15:59.208
was extremely difficult.

15:59.291 --> 16:01.794
If it is that hard,

16:01.877 --> 16:04.964
why did they say
we had to take the toughest route?

16:06.757 --> 16:08.384
It'll be fine now.

16:08.467 --> 16:11.136
Thanks to the Science Hero's GPS,

16:11.220 --> 16:13.180
our current location is clear.

16:13.263 --> 16:15.057
Yes, but still,

16:15.140 --> 16:19.061
how is that GPS working so far
in this stone world?

16:20.521 --> 16:23.857
We're keeping in contact
with Ruri back in Japan.

16:25.484 --> 16:28.529
What's this?
There are so many hourglasses!

16:29.154 --> 16:30.864
With the sundials and hourglasses,

16:30.948 --> 16:33.534
we can communicate Japanese time

16:33.617 --> 16:36.745
as accurately as possible
to everyone on board.

16:36.829 --> 16:38.205
Communicate?

16:38.288 --> 16:39.540
In that case…

16:39.623 --> 16:42.292
Chrome can express his true feelings
from this far offshore!

16:42.376 --> 16:45.129
What's up with them?

16:45.212 --> 16:48.674
You're getting ridiculously off-topic.
They can't just make a phone call.

16:49.258 --> 16:50.968
Then, how?

16:51.051 --> 16:54.847
We can't make phone calls,
but we can somehow get a shortwave signal.

16:54.930 --> 16:57.433
That's why we can get
a time update every hour.

16:58.976 --> 16:59.810
Look.

17:00.602 --> 17:03.063
Right now,
it's ten in the morning over there.

17:03.814 --> 17:05.524
If we know what time it is,

17:05.607 --> 17:09.153
I can just precisely count the minutes.

17:09.737 --> 17:13.615
Come to think of it, Senku,
they called you a human chronometer…

17:14.199 --> 17:18.162
But I can't understand
why we can know where we are

17:18.245 --> 17:20.164
just by knowing what time it is.

17:20.247 --> 17:21.540
It's okay.

17:21.623 --> 17:24.918
Modern adults cannot
understand it really well, either.

17:25.794 --> 17:28.630
The sun is at its highest point!

17:28.714 --> 17:31.258
On this boat, it is exactly midday.

17:33.343 --> 17:35.262
The time gap from Tokyo
shows our horizontal position,

17:36.221 --> 17:38.265
and the position of the sun
shows our vertical.

17:38.348 --> 17:41.226
That means,
the current location of Perseus

17:41.310 --> 17:42.436
&lrm;is here!

17:44.521 --> 17:47.608
I didn't get the theory,
but finally I've understood.

17:47.691 --> 17:50.903
If we know our horizontal and vertical
coordinates, we know where we are!

17:50.986 --> 17:53.405
Traveling science is amazing!

17:53.489 --> 17:56.658
We can understand everything
just by looking at the sun!

17:56.742 --> 17:59.912
Exactly! As long as it is sunny,

17:59.995 --> 18:02.581
it's going to be
a smooth sailing trip to America!

18:09.713 --> 18:12.716
Just right after Senku said that.
Unbelievable!

18:12.800 --> 18:15.677
The day Senku was born,

18:15.761 --> 18:18.639
his 100 skill points were distributed
among intelligence,

18:18.722 --> 18:21.308
spirit and effort,
leaving zero points for luck.

18:23.102 --> 18:27.189
We cannot see the sun this way.

18:29.108 --> 18:32.694
In times like this, we have no choice
but to sail based on my intuition.

18:32.778 --> 18:35.739
If this keeps up for days,
it's going to cause a serious delay.

18:36.323 --> 18:39.701
If the sun doesn't come out,
we'll get lost in the sea.

18:40.285 --> 18:44.915
If only we had sunstones
like in the Viking story…

18:50.129 --> 18:51.130
This is it!

18:51.713 --> 18:56.135
This is a new stone I got from
the Skarn deposit, a natural treasure box.

18:56.218 --> 18:59.555
Kohaku, can you use your gorilla might
to turn this into a perfect square?

19:00.389 --> 19:03.725
I wonder if there's a pretty gem
inside of it.

19:03.809 --> 19:06.770
If you're giving it to Ruri,
I'm definitely at your service.

19:09.565 --> 19:11.775
It is somehow neat, but…

19:11.859 --> 19:13.902
Isn't it an average whitish stone?

19:14.528 --> 19:16.655
I thought so at first, too.

19:16.738 --> 19:19.616
But, if you look through it…

19:20.784 --> 19:22.119
Something's off…

19:22.953 --> 19:24.788
I can see two of you!

19:25.873 --> 19:28.292
It is an interesting stone somehow,

19:28.375 --> 19:31.086
but I wonder
if Ruri will actually like it.

19:31.170 --> 19:33.964
What are you talking about?

19:34.882 --> 19:39.803
<i>Someday, I might find</i>
<i>something that's truly amazing.</i>

19:39.887 --> 19:41.763
<i>That's why I check everything out.</i>

19:42.556 --> 19:45.225
That's what being a scientist
is all about!

19:48.061 --> 19:49.062
Hey, look.

19:49.146 --> 19:52.858
The mark doesn't look like
it's doubled here, right?

19:53.442 --> 19:55.569
Could this over here be the sun?

19:55.652 --> 19:57.738
I can't see anything
with all those clouds.

19:58.906 --> 20:00.407
Clouds are not a problem.

20:00.490 --> 20:04.161
I just need to point this at the sun
at the right angle, like this…

20:05.078 --> 20:06.371
Look, it's aligned now!

20:06.455 --> 20:09.124
The intensity of both marks
is slowly matching.

20:09.833 --> 20:11.919
I don't really know
why this happens, though.

20:12.628 --> 20:13.462
That means…

20:14.171 --> 20:16.715
Yes, right where the stone is pointing

20:17.299 --> 20:19.468
is the sun
we're not supposed to be seeing!

20:19.551 --> 20:21.845
YOU FOUND THE SUNSTONE

20:22.429 --> 20:23.931
It's Calcite!

20:24.514 --> 20:26.683
<i>It is a natural prism.</i>

20:26.767 --> 20:29.144
<i>It refracts light</i>
<i>and divides it into two parts.</i>

20:29.728 --> 20:33.482
You mean, it aligns
if it's pointed towards sunlight?

20:33.565 --> 20:35.817
In basic terms, yes.

20:37.236 --> 20:38.487
I've heard of it.

20:39.571 --> 20:42.783
<i>While investigating Viking shipwrecks</i>
<i>at the bottom of the ocean,</i>

20:42.866 --> 20:45.786
<i>I kept encountering calcite stones</i>
<i>for some reason.</i>

20:46.453 --> 20:49.748
<i>The reason the Vikings</i>
<i>were carrying those stones</i>

20:49.831 --> 20:51.792
<i>has been a total enigma until now.</i>

20:52.376 --> 20:55.254
Perhaps the sunstones were real after all,

20:55.337 --> 20:58.632
and not some kind of magic or legend.

21:00.342 --> 21:03.387
That's right!
And now they've transcended 4000 years.

21:04.554 --> 21:06.974
Just like the Vikings did,

21:07.057 --> 21:09.393
we're steering
towards the American continent

21:09.476 --> 21:12.187
with the very same
unwavering power of science!

21:16.066 --> 21:17.901
<i>What will we make next using science?</i>

22:02.529 --> 22:03.447
Landfall…

22:08.577 --> 22:11.663
Finally… We have arrived!

22:11.747 --> 22:15.917
-We've reached America!
-We've reached America!

